// RECOMMENDED encoding for the helpdesk grant's temporal access, per this pack's existing
// gate decision (schema-mapping.md, "Temporal access: caveat vs. native expiration", "The
// gate decision"). That decision was made once, for temporal-access, and is applied here
// unchanged rather than re-litigated: native expiration is the recommended default for a
// temporal-access condition carried over from an OpenFGA source, and caveat (schema.zed) is
// the documented alternative for a call site that genuinely needs "was this valid at time T"
// rather than "is this valid right now" -- which is exactly this store's own case (see below).
//
// schema.zed (the caveat form) remains the only artifact this store's harness ritual
// validates -- not because it is the better production choice, but because it is the only
// one this pack's own tooling (zed validate / the migration-harness) can verify end to end,
// and because this store's own source assertions are themselves fixed-instant ("was this
// grant valid at 2024-01-01T00:10:00Z"), not real-clock-relative -- reproducing them with
// native expiration would require either sleeping in real time or asserting against dates
// arbitrarily far in the past/future relative to whenever the suite runs, neither of which
// tests the same claim the source does. See schema-mapping.md's "Why the harness only
// verifies the caveat form" for the general version of this argument.
//
// Verified live (SpiceDB v1.56.0, separate keyspace/token from schema.zed's): `use
// expiration` is required (dropping it reproduces the documented trap exactly -- "could not
// lookup caveat `expiration` for relation `helpdesk_member`: caveat with name `expiration`
// not found"); with the flag, WriteSchema and zed validate both accept this file directly,
// no compile step. A relationship written with `--expiration-time` 6s in the future checked
// true and appeared in `zed relationship read` immediately after write; ~8s later (inside
// SpiceDB's GC interval, so nothing was physically reclaimed) it checked false and had
// vanished from `zed relationship read` entirely -- gone from every read path the instant
// the timestamp passes, independent of GC, matching schema-mapping.md's documented mechanism
// exactly.

use expiration

definition user {}

definition employee {}

definition application {}

definition system {
	relation admin: employee | application

	permission admin__perm = admin
}

definition organization {
	relation system: system
	relation admin__direct: user
	relation member: user
	relation helpdesk_member: employee with expiration

	permission admin = (admin__direct + system->admin__perm)
	permission can_create_project = (admin + member)
	permission helpdesk_member__perm = helpdesk_member
}

definition project {
	relation organization: organization
	relation owner: user
	relation editor__direct: user
	relation viewer__direct: user

	permission editor = (editor__direct + owner + organization->admin)
	permission viewer = (viewer__direct + editor + organization->helpdesk_member__perm)
}

definition task {
	relation project: project
	relation owner: user
	relation editor__direct: user
	relation viewer__direct: user

	permission editor = (editor__direct + project->editor)
	permission viewer = (viewer__direct + editor + project->viewer)
}

// Illustrative write for this store's own fixture data (issued_at + valid_for,
// computed once at conversion time into one absolute timestamp, per the gate decision's
// recorded consequence -- the source's separate issued_at/valid_for values are not
// reconstructable from it afterward):
//
//   zed relationship touch organization:acme helpdesk_member employee:john --expiration-time 2024-01-01T01:00:00Z   # issued_at (00:00:00Z) + 1h
//
// That grant is expired relative to any real clock past 2024 -- on a live server today,
// `organization:acme#helpdesk_member@employee:john` (and anything reached through it) checks
// false and is absent from `zed relationship read`, which is expected and is exactly the
// scope-narrowing the gate decision names: this file can answer "is john's grant valid right
// now," never "was it valid at 2024-01-01T00:10:00Z."
